ary Care Team is the central coordinating entity enabling multidisciplinary care\",\"explanation\":\"The Multidisciplinary Care Team is described as a coordinated group of doctors, medical specialists, and allied health professionals who communicate and work collaboratively to provide integrated patient care, specifically including bladder cancer care across active therapy and follow-up. Its responsibilities include diagnosis, joint planning, delivery, and monitoring of treatment, as well as aligning symptom control, supportive services, and discussions of the patient’s goals and preferences. [Data: Entities (4336)]\\n\\nA direct relationship links the Multidisciplinary Care Team to the concept of multidisciplinary care, specifying that multidisciplinary care is the model that occurs when the team works and communicates together to help the patient. This positions the “team” as the operational mechanism for the “care model.” [Data: Relationships (9094); Entities (4336, 4351)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Multidisciplinary care is explicitly defined as a communication- and coordination-driven care model\",\"explanation\":\"Multidisciplinary care is defined as a coordinated model in which multiple providers communicate and work as a team to help the patient across diagnosis and treatment, including goal-setting and coordinated appointments. This definition highlights that coordination is both clinical (shared decisions and goal-setting) and logistical (aligned scheduling). [Data: Entities (4351)]\\n\\nThe team-to-model linkage reinforces that this is not an abstract ideal but an enacted approach: multidisciplinary care “occurs” when the multidisciplinary care team works and communicates together. This emphasizes sustained collaboration as the distinguishing feature of the community’s care structure. [Data: Relationships (9094); Entities (4336, 4351)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The care team composition spans specialty oncology, symptom management, and allied health support\",\"explanatio